edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101031 #edb/ref 1045 "Reducing ODEs to First-Order Linear by Substitution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101031 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101031 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101031 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cf46c580-819b-5052-9426-d777358df814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101760 #edb/ref 1061 24251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "caa22dea-6722-5c27-aa99-c035d6beef32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101761 #edb/ref 1061 24252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d4ce90c3-1349-518a-a9d8-ac07ccd89f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101762 #edb/ref 1061 24253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0cc9be1-78d0-5fe0-bab5-2513035bccbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101777 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101777 #edb/ref 1061 25429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101777 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01d942ef-7638-5c9e-9bff-ff212769b885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101777 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101992 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the first-order differential equation\n\n$$\n2y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+y^2=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that it is *not* a first-order linear equation because it cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x) y = Q(x).$ In fact, this equation is an example of a *nonlinear* first-order differential equation.\n\nWe can sometimes transform a nonlinear differential equation into a linear differential equation using a substitution. If we can do this, then we can apply the method of integrating factors to solve it.\n\nTo transform the above equation into a linear differential equation, we can use the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}z} {\\textrm{d}x}=2y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}z} {\\textrm{d}x}+z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear! So, we can use the method of integrating factors. The given equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x.$ We then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=e^x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}{e}^{x}+z{e}^{x} & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =x{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now integrate with respect to $x,$ using integration by parts to integrate the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =(x-1){e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =x-1+C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^2.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x-1+C{e}^{-x} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{x-1+C{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101992 #edb/ref 1065 4060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101992 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60bfb242-6329-5820-adf6-d96d5c970d57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101992 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281731 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281731 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281731 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04c29e1e-72f5-5778-9190-14096e99274a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282106 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282106 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282106 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ede90c1-9985-5fbb-b71c-2a2ac9b675be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282358 #edb/ref 1080 "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d9b7195-26cd-5b20-b122-b93d7703dc7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282792 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282792 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282792 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "800de7ca-c36f-55ec-816a-c702b91b521f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283481 #edb/ref 1080 "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283481 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283481 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ccf94ef5-4cf5-5987-a03e-76e204f1250d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186281731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284370 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4bbf1a2-d136-5527-93ec-335bba3c14e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{-3}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5525f3f9-de37-524f-895a-dbf6bcc144ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284573 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-3}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3{z}^{4/3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{4/3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{z}^{1/3}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3z\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346678 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9766a5b4-5490-5eee-b3f1-783a74ac63b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347024 #edb/ref 1080 "x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c19759f0-6bc3-5195-b4be-cff9483eff83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347125 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347125 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347125 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb13d478-5fe0-5ee0-a40c-fdbff793af9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347354 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347354 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347354 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3cc6ff1-ca27-59e7-9d71-4cf9d0a51b46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347371 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e57c2fb9-40b2-59fa-bc73-10dac5d1e9b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186347371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347708 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3d72a2b9-800f-5dd2-b389-9f6fdaf44620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{-2}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y(1-x{y}^{2})\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "528aceec-87ad-5025-88b0-8b8742b0b21b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348282 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(1-x{z}^{-1}) \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(x{z}^{-1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}z(x{z}^{-1}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
